{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSideExponents","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSideExponents","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.Circle"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSide.norm_sq_eq_q_sub_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSide.norm_sq_eq_q_sub_one","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ZeroSideExponents.lean#L157-L168","doc":"The exponents therefore lie on the circle of radius `√(q−1)`: since\n`normSq t = |t|²`, the statement above is `|t|² = q − 1`.\n\nRecorded because that same `√(q−1)` is the numerator of the `∞`-side leading\nblocks `±√(q−1)/q^{3/4}`. Whether the coincidence of that factor across the two\nsingular points is structural or accidental is **not** settled here — it is an\nobservation, and naming it prematurely is exactly the habit this arc has been\ncorrecting. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSide.normSq_eq_q_sub_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSide.normSq_eq_q_sub_one","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ZeroSideExponents.lean#L145-L155","doc":"**For a conjugate pair, `|t|² = q − 1`.**\n\n`t * conj t = |t|²` and Vieta gives the product of the roots as `q − 1`, so the\ntwo exponents lie on the circle of radius `√(q−1)`. Measured at the substrate:\n`|t| = 0.33163018974662244642` against `√(q−1) = 0.33163018974662244642`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSide.conjugate_pair_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSide.conjugate_pair_iff","kind":"theorem","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ZeroSideExponents.lean#L127-L143","doc":"**The exponents are a conjugate (non-real) pair exactly for `1 < q < 5`.**\n\nThe substrate `q₀ ≈ 1.10998` sits well inside this window, so the `0`-side\nexponents there are genuinely complex — the Fuchsian side is not diagonalisable\nover `ℝ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSide.discriminant_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSide.discriminant_eq","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ZeroSideExponents.lean#L123-L125","doc":"**The discriminant factors as `(q−1)(q−5)`.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSide.vieta","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSide.vieta","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ZeroSideExponents.lean#L105-L121","doc":"**Vieta.** If `t₁, t₂` are the two exponents then their product is `q − 1`\nand their sum is `−(q − 1)`. Stated as: the polynomial factors as\n`(t − t₁)(t − t₂)` exactly when those two relations hold. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSide.zeroSideIndicial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1ZeroSide.zeroSideIndicial","kind":"def","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ZeroSideExponents.lean#L101-L103","doc":"The `0`-side indicial polynomial `t² + (q−1)t + (q−1)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}